<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Freiburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1457</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Albert-Ludwigs-Universität Freiburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of Freiburg is a public university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiburg-im-breisgau">Freiburg im Breisgau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, founded in 1457 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-vii-archduke-of-austria">Archduke Albert</a> VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, then ruler of Further Austria. One of the oldest universities in Germany, it remained Catholic during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> in 1805. Its teachers have included the philosophers Edmund Husserl and Martin Heidegger.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-freiburg.jpg" alt="University of Freiburg" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: University of Freiburg Vector: Calestyo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siegel_der_Albert-Ludwigs-Universit%C3%A4t_Freiburg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
